-{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750098517,"sessionID":"ses_163069809ffeuCPl3U2riKHvxp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_lI9LzIvlNcXQpdksGpcqd0oj","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":403,"limit":130},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n403: ## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n404: \n405: Implementation has three sub-steps.\n406: The test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\n407: That rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\n408: Step 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\n409: When Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\n410: Rule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\n411: After Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n412: \n413: - Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n414: - Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n415: - Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n416: - Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n417: - Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n418: - Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n419: - Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n420: - Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n421: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n422: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n423: - Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n424: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n425: \n426: ### Step 6a - Define contract\n427: \n428: - Owner: orchestrator\n429: - Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n430: - Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n431: - Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n432: - Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n433: - Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n434:   1. `parent_level_id`\n435:   2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n436:   3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n437:   4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n438:   5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n439:   6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n440:   7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n441:   8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n442: - Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n443: - Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n444: \n445: ### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n446: \n447: - Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n448: - Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n449: - Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n450: - Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n451: - Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n452: - Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n453: - Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n454: - Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n455: - Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n456: - Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n457: - Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n458: - Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n459: - Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n460: - Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n461: - Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n462: - Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n463: - Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n464: - Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n465: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n466: - Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n467: - Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n468: - Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n469: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n470: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n471: - Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n472: \n473: ### Step 6c - Write code\n474: \n475: - Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n476: - Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n477: - Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n478: - Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n479: - Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n480: - Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n481: - Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n482: - Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n483: - Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n484: - Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n485: - Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n486: - Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n487: - Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n488: - Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n489: - Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n490: - Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n491: - Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n492: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n493: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n494: - Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n495: - Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n496: - Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n497: - Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n498: - Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n499: - Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n500: - Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n501: - Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n502: - Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n503: - Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n504: - Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n505: - Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n506: - Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n507: - Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n508: - Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n509: - Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n510: - Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n511: - Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n512: \n513: ## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n514: \n515: The implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n516: \n517: - Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n518: - Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n519: - Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n520: - Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n521: - The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n522: - `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n523: - `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n524: - `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n525: - The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n526: - If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n527: \n528: ## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n529: \n530: - These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n531: - Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n532: - The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.\n\n(Showing lines 403-532 of 652. Use offset=533 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n- Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n\n### Step 6a - Define contract\n\n- Owner: orchestrator\n- Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n- Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n- Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n- Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n- Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n  1. `parent_level_id`\n  2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n  3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n  4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n  5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n  6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n  7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n  8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n- Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n- Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n\n### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n\n- Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n- Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n- Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n- Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n- Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n- Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n- Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n- Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n- Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n- Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n- Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n- Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n- Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n- Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n- Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n- Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n- Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n- Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n- Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n\n### Step 6c - Write code\n\n- Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n- Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n- Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n- Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n- Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n- Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n- Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n- Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n- Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n- Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n- Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n- Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n- Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n- Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n- Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n- Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n- Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n- Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n- Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n- Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n- Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n- Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n- Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n- Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n- Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n- Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n- Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n\n## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n\nThe implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n\n- Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n- Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n- Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n- Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n- The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n- `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n- `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n- `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n- The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n- If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n\n## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n\n- These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n- Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n- The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.","lineStart":403,"lineEnd":532,"totalLines":652,"truncated":true}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780750098506,"end":1780750098513}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_04e80d1eac79944f016a2417124f688193ad698d40254078b6"}},"id":"prt_e9cfa1f9e001G2gC2Dme24I5xL","sessionID":"ses_163069809ffeuCPl3U2riKHvxp","messageID":"msg_e9cfa0acb001qQexo92109LWl3"}}
+{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750098517,"sessionID":"ses_163069809ffeuCPl3U2riKHvxp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_lI9LzIvlNcXQpdksGpcqd0oj","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":403,"limit":130},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n403: ## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n404: \n405: Implementation has three sub-steps.\n406: The test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\n407: That rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\n408: Step 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\n409: When Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\n410: Rule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\n411: After Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n412: \n413: - Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n414: - Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n415: - Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `planning/research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `planning/research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n416: - Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n417: - Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n418: - Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n419: - Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n420: - Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n421: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n422: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n423: - Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n424: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n425: \n426: ### Step 6a - Define contract\n427: \n428: - Owner: orchestrator\n429: - Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n430: - Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n431: - Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n432: - Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n433: - Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n434:   1. `parent_level_id`\n435:   2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n436:   3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n437:   4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n438:   5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n439:   6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n440:   7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n441:   8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n442: - Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n443: - Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n444: \n445: ### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n446: \n447: - Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `planning/risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n448: - Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n449: - Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n450: - Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n451: - Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n452: - Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n453: - Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n454: - Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n455: - Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n456: - Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n457: - Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n458: - Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n459: - Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n460: - Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n461: - Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n462: - Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n463: - Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n464: - Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n465: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n466: - Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n467: - Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n468: - Output: `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n469: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n470: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n471: - Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n472: \n473: ### Step 6c - Write code\n474: \n475: - Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n476: - Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n477: - Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n478: - Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n479: - Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n480: - Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n481: - Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n482: - Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n483: - Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n484: - Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n485: - Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n486: - Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n487: - Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n488: - Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n489: - Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n490: - Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n491: - Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n492: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n493: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n494: - Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n495: - Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n496: - Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n497: - Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n498: - Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n499: - Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n500: - Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n501: - Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n502: - Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n503: - Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n504: - Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n505: - Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n506: - Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n507: - Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n508: - Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n509: - Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n510: - Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n511: - Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n512: \n513: ## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n514: \n515: The implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n516: \n517: - Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n518: - Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n519: - Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n520: - Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n521: - The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n522: - `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n523: - `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n524: - `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n525: - The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n526: - If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n527: \n528: ## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n529: \n530: - These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n531: - Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n532: - The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.\n\n(Showing lines 403-532 of 652. Use offset=533 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `planning/research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `planning/research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `planning/research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `planning/research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n- Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n\n### Step 6a - Define contract\n\n- Owner: orchestrator\n- Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n- Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n- Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n- Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n- Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n  1. `parent_level_id`\n  2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n  3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n  4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n  5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n  6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n  7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n  8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n- Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n- Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n\n### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n\n- Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `planning/risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n- Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n- Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n- Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n- Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n- Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n- Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n- Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n- Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n- Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n- Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n- Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n- Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n- Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n- Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n- Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n- Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n- Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n- Output: `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n\n### Step 6c - Write code\n\n- Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n- Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n- Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n- Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n- Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n- Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n- Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n- Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n- Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n- Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n- Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n- Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n- Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n- Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n- Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n- Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n- Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n- Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n- Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n- Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n- Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n- Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n- Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n- Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n- Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n- Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n- Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n\n## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n\nThe implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n\n- Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n- Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n- Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n- Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n- The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n- `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n- `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n- `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n- The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n- If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n\n## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n\n- These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n- Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n- The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.","lineStart":403,"lineEnd":532,"totalLines":652,"truncated":true}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780750098506,"end":1780750098513}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_04e80d1eac79944f016a2417124f688193ad698d40254078b6"}},"id":"prt_e9cfa1f9e001G2gC2Dme24I5xL","sessionID":"ses_163069809ffeuCPl3U2riKHvxp","messageID":"msg_e9cfa0acb001qQexo92109LWl3"}}
